Yes, it is possible to call outgoing web service from T-SQL stored procedure, but it is not considered as a good practice as it may cause performance degradation of your SQL Server. Instead, it is recommended to put such business logic in the application layer.

If you really need to achieve this, you can use the "sp_OAMethod" to make the HTTP request to web service. To do this, firstly you need to enable the OLE Automation Procedures on SQL Server by running the following commands.

```sql
EXEC sp_configure 'show advanced options', 1;
RECONFIGURE;
EXEC sp_configure 'Ole Automation Procedures', 1;
RECONFIGURE;
```
Then, create a stored procedure for making the HTTP requests. Below is an example.

```sql
CREATE PROCEDURE WebServiceCall
AS 
BEGIN 

DECLARE @obj INT, @url VARCHAR(200), @responseText VARCHAR(8000); 

EXEC sp_OACreate 'MSXML2.ServerXMLHTTP', @obj OUT; 

SET @url='http://webservice-url/';

EXEC sp_OAMethod @obj, 'open', NULL, 'get', @url, 'false';

EXEC sp_OAMethod @obj, 'send'; 

EXEC sp_OAMethod @obj, 'responseText', @responseText OUTPUT; 

EXEC sp_OADestroy @obj; 

SELECT @responseText; 

END
```
Please note using "sp_OA" procedures and the "MSXML2.ServerXMLHTTP" object requires permissions and settings that might not be suitable or safe for your SQL Server environment. Use it carefully and understand the potential impacts this could have on your server.

