Sure, here is a Java implementation of Radix Sort algorithm capable of sorting both integer and floating-point numbers, including negative values.

We will use two methods, the `radixSort` function to implement the radix sort algorithm and a `normalize` function to convert negative and floating point numbers into suitable positive integers for the radix sort.

```java
public class RadixSort {

    // A utility function to get maximum value in arr[]
    static int getMax(Integer arr[], int n) {
        int mx = arr[0];
        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++)
            if (arr[i] > mx)
                mx = arr[i];
        return mx;
    }

    static void countSort(Integer arr[], int n, int exp) {
        int output[] = new int[n]; // output array
        int i;
        int count[] = new int[10];

        // Initialize count array as 0
        for (i = 0; i < 10; i++)
            count[i] = 0;

        // Store count of occurrences in count[]
        for (i = 0; i < n; i++)
            count[(arr[i] / exp) % 10]++;

        for (i = 1; i < 10; i++)
            count[i] += count[i - 1];

        for (i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            output[count[(arr[i] / exp) % 10] - 1] = arr[i];
            count[(arr[i] / exp) % 10]--;
        }

        for (i = 0; i < n; i++)
            arr[i] = output[i];
    }

    // Radix Sort
    static void radixSort(Integer arr[], int n) {
        int m = getMax(arr, n);

        for (int exp = 1; m / exp > 0; exp *= 10)
            countSort(arr, n, exp);
    }

   // Function to convert elements to suitable integer for radix sort
   static Integer[] normalize(double arr[], int n) {
        double min = arr[0];

        // Find min value of the array
        for(int i=1; i<n; i++) {
            if(arr[i] < min)
                min = arr[i];
        }

        Integer result[] = new Integer[n];

        int shift = 0;

        if(min < 0) {
            shift = (int)Math.ceil(Math.abs(min));
        }

        // Normalize elements and convert to integer
        for(int i=0; i<n; i++) {
            result[i] = (int)((arr[i] + shift) * 10000); // Multiply by 10000 to maintain 4 decimal places (to support floating point)
        }

        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String args[]) {
        double arr[] = {-21.2, -10.0, -18.3, 7.4, 60.2, -20.2, 70.0};
        int n = arr.length;

        Integer normalizedArr[] = normalize(arr, n);

        radixSort(normalizedArr, n);

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
            System.out.print(((double)normalizedArr[i])/10000 + " ");
    }
}
```
This program works by normalizing the array to convert negative and floating point numbers into positive integers. After sorting the array with radix sort, the final array is printed after converting back into the floating point representation.

Please note that the current implementation supports floating point numbers upto 4 decimal places, you can adjust the multiplication factor in the `normalize` function to support more/less decimal places.

```python
def longest_increasing_subsequence(numbers):
    """
    Returns the length of the longest increasing subsequence in the list of integers.

    :param numbers: List of integers
    :return: Length of the longest increasing subsequence
    """
    if not numbers:
        return 0

    dp = [1] * len(numbers)

    for i in range(1, len(numbers)):
        for j in range(i):
            if numbers[i] > numbers[j]:
                dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[j] + 1)

    return max(dp)
