Solving this problem involves multiple steps: sorting entries based on the count of their alphanumeric characters, ordering them in decreasing order, and writing the SQL statement. Assume that the table's name is 'table_name'.

Firstly, in most SQL flavors, there is not a built-in function that could count the alphanumeric characters. However, you can create a user-defined function to achieve this. 

For instance, in PostgreSQL, we could create a function like so:

```SQL
CREATE FUNCTION count_alpha_numeric_characters(text) RETURNS integer AS $$
DECLARE
  char_count integer := 0;
  text_length integer := length($1);
  current_character text;
BEGIN
  FOR i IN 1..text_length LOOP
    current_character := substring($1 from i for 1);
    IF current_character ~ '[A-Za-z0-9]' THEN
      char_count := char_count + 1;
    END IF;
  END LOOP;
  RETURN char_count;
END;
$$ LANGUAGE plpgsql;
```

Note that this function loops over each character of the input string and increments a counter if the character is alphanumeric.

After you have this function ready, you can use it in your SQL statement:

```SQL
SELECT title,
       count_alpha_numeric_characters(title) AS alpha_numeric_count
FROM table_name
ORDER BY alpha_numeric_count DESC;
```

This SQL statement lists the 'title' entries from the 'table_name' table and the count of alphanumeric characters in these titles, sorted in descending order based on the count of alphanumeric characters.

Please keep in mind, the actual implementation may vary depending on your SQL flavor and version. For example, in MySQL, the implementation to create a user-defined function may look different, and in some database management systems, creating functions may not be allowed at all.

