Here is a basic Java program that accomplishes this:

```java
public class LargeNumberAddition {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            String a = "1098120912830912803192810831201039218390218";  // Replace with your own numbers
            String b = "1238918391823018230192830198231098210398103";

            validate(a);
            validate(b);

            String sum = add(a, b);

            System.out.println("The sum is: " + sum);

        } catch (NumberFormatException e) {
            System.out.println("Error: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }

    public static void validate(String num) throws NumberFormatException {
        if (!num.matches("\\d*")) { 
            throw new NumberFormatException("Invalid number: " + num);
        }
    }

    public static String add(String a, String b) {
        StringBuilder sum = new StringBuilder();

        int i = a.length() - 1, j = b.length() - 1, carry = 0;
        while (i >= 0 || j >= 0 || carry > 0) {
            int digitA = i < 0 ? 0 : a.charAt(i--) - '0';
            int digitB = j < 0 ? 0 : b.charAt(j--) - '0';

            int digitsSum = digitA + digitB + carry;
            carry = digitsSum / 10;
            digitsSum %= 10;

            sum.append(digitsSum);
        }

        return sum.reverse().toString();
    }
}
```

The program reads two large numbers as strings (you can replace 'a' and 'b' with your own numbers). It then validates if the strings can represent integers and line-wise adds them if they do. Custom exception is thrown if the string is not a valid number representation. Program will catch this exception and print a meaningful error message. If the validation is true, the add method is called to calculate the sum of two string numbers digit by digit. The digit sum is put into the StringBuilder from the end, and in the end it will be reversed to get the right order of the sum, which will be printed out in the main function. 

The integers are validated using a regular expression (`\\d*`) that checks if a string consists of only digits 0-9. 

The add method processes the strings from right (end) to left (start), simulating the manual process of adding numbers. The carry is computed and passed on to the next iteration.

