&lt;p&gt;
In the Hundred Acre Wood, Christopher Robin and his friend Pooh Bear have a fondness for the game of Poohsticks, so much so that they have devised an extended version of the game that prolongs the duration before a winner is declared and they must return home for their tea. The game commences in the usual manner with both participants releasing a stick simultaneously on the upstream side of a bridge. However, the game does not conclude when one of the sticks appears on the downstream side. Instead, they retrieve their sticks from the water and reintroduce them on the upstream side. The game reaches its conclusion only when one of the sticks appears from beneath the bridge ahead of the other, having also 'lapped' the other stick - meaning, it has completed one additional journey under the bridge compared to the other stick.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
On a specific day of engaging in this game, the duration for a stick to traverse under the bridge fluctuates between a minimum of 30 seconds and a maximum of 60 seconds. The time required to retrieve a stick from the water and reintroduce it on the other side is 5 seconds. The current beneath the bridge possesses the peculiar characteristic that the sticks' journey time is always an integral number of seconds, and it is equally probable to emerge at any of the possible times between 30 and 60 seconds (inclusive). Under these conditions, the expected duration for playing a single game is 1036.15 seconds (rounded to 2 decimal places). This duration is measured from the moment of releasing the sticks for the first time, to the moment where the winning stick appears from under the bridge having lapped the other.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
The stream's flow rate varies each day, but retains the property that the journey time in seconds is equally distributed amongst the integers from a minimum, $n$, to a maximum, $m$, inclusive. Let the expected duration of play in seconds be denoted as $E(m,n)$. Hence, $E(60,30)=1036.15...$
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
Let $S(k)=\sum_{m=2}^k\sum_{n=1}^{m-1}E(m,n)$.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
For instance, $S(5)=7722.82$ rounded to 2 decimal places.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
Determine the value of $S(100)$ and provide your answer rounded to 2 decimal places.
&lt;/p&gt;

